I produced concrete before/after examples showing the real format the historian receives, the forged line, and the sanitized result.

U: so, assumption is that model would get toolcall output which mimicry under historian summary and put it into history block? why isn't toolcall output is not wrapped then
I corrected the chain: tool *output* already can't enter the history block (the chunk builder drops results, keeps only TC stubs). The hole is the tool call's **input** — the `description` field. The full attack chain: page poisons the main model's description param → model copies it verbatim (legitimate) → flattener splices it raw into a TC line → historian sees a fake U: turn → compartment published → region serves it forever. The fix lives at the splice (structure→text conversion), same principle as HTML escaping at render time.

U: anyway, your solution 2 solves first case also, just \n newlines and all of the special symbols
I merged into one unified sanitizer at every paste site: fold line-breaking whitespace (`\n \r \u2028 \u2029` → space, matching the Rust expansion path's existing precedent at `historian_tool_template.rs:188-206`) + defang the marker token (`] U:` / `] A:` / `] TC:` sequences get one character inserted). Everything else passes untouched. The TS lane already has `normalizeText` (`\s+` → space) applied to user/assistant text — the bug is only that the three TC splices in `read-session-formatting.ts` bypass it. Implementation: run every raw paste through the one sanitizer with marker-defang added, delete the bypass. Laws: planted forgery must arrive as a single defanged line (both lanes), planted inline marker must not remain byte-identical, real builder-emitted U: lines unchanged (control).

U: i guess it's a separate bugfix, we may also consider reporting it to upstream
