## 23785-23796 · OpenViking working_memory disable investigation
The user asked for a plain-language explanation of what `working_memory.enabled=false` means in OpenViking, mapped to MC feature equivalents. I explained: WM is their running session summary (one mutable doc rewritten at every commit); turning it off removes the summaries but keeps the raw tape and long-term memories. The three components lacking when off: the archive overview (equivalent to MC compartment blocks), the retention checkpoint (equivalent to what an MC fold leaves in place of dropped messages — with WM off, the drop has zero replacement), and the rolling WM doc itself (no MC equivalent — MC appends immutable per-span summaries; WM is one mutable current-task memo).

U: so and what's their reason to disable it? it's kinda one of the main MC features

They never published a "why" — the default flip ships as a bare product decision with no rationale doc. But the issue tracker shows the shape: (1) the documented config key `memory_policy.working_memory.enabled: true` was silently ignored by the server (#6207, Oct 17, still open) — a context-management default whose state can silently diverge from operator belief gets turned off; (2) takeover destroyed transcripts — "Some old OV-managed conversations no longer have a full host transcript" — MC's cardinal sin (verbatim store first); (3) the takeover plumbing was fragile (plugins polling the server for summaries). WM still runs by default inside VikingBot (their own harness where they own the whole wire). The pattern: summarization survives where the summarizer owns the transcript end-to-end, dies as a default where it summarizes someone else's session remotely.

U: well, yeah, like couple of moth before i can't remember them having any session management at all

I verified via git history: repo created 2026-01-05; `08-session.md` exists since the first commit (Jan 29) but that's DB-flavored bucket storage (create → add_message → commit, raw messages.jsonl kept). Session management in the MC sense — the compaction machinery — is autumn: `working_memory.py`, `archive_store.py`, `checkpoints.py`, `tool_output_externalizer.py` were extracted out of `session.py` on Sept 24 (#5363). WM/checkpoint/takeover work kept landing through October. Then v0.5.0 (~mid-October) flips WM to default-off. The Oct 30 WM commit is co-authored `Co-authored-by: TRAE CLI` — substantially AI-authored, churned fast. MC's compaction physics has eleven rounds of adversarial review and field receipts; theirs is a September feature that by November its own makers ship switched off by default. Strongest data point for note #93's from-scratch argument: the wire discipline has to precede the summarization feature.

## 23797-23802 · invizipburu offload path design ruling
U: well, than it's a storage for memories and session data which our assumed invizipburu might have offloading path to

The user reframed OV's role for invizipburu: not as a summarizer (their weakness) but as cold storage (their strength — stable ids, canonical writes, typed archive, TTL/ACL, tiering). I analyzed the offload role and identified three preconditions MC's laws would demand: (1) `get(ref)` must return bytes-identical to `put` — OV's write path canonically rewrites sidecars and silently drops unknown fields, so verbatim law needs red-first proof; (2) TTL must be truly off — their sample configs ship `ttl: {sessions: 7}`; (3) zero model calls on the archival path — extraction pipeline must be detachable. I proposed defining the offload seam as a pluggable cold-tier interface with OV as adapter candidate, never as substrate.

U: "define the offload seam as a pluggable cold-tier interface" that's exactly what i meant, even if we even start today it's still wouldn't be on a table for a while, local-first mechanisms have to work properly before any pluggable storage

Owner ruling: local-first correctness gates any pluggable storage. The byte-fidelity contract for the seam can only be derived from a working local store — you need to know exactly which reads the hot path makes, what "verbatim" means per row class, and what the durability boundary actually is, or the interface bakes in whatever semantics the first external backend happens to have. OV are the cautionary case: their remote/takeover layer got built ahead of the local discipline, and by the time store-layer rules matured, the product had to walk the upper layer back to off. Sequencing law recorded in note #86: local store first, contract from its observed behavior, adapter as a test against an already-honest system. OV stays filed as adapter candidate with its three falsification gates; nothing on the current board moves on it.

