## 23825-23842 · 2026-11-04 · Splice sanitizer demo and design rationale validation
I built an honest demo of the splice sanitizer: pulled the pre-fix module from the base commit next to the fixed one, ran both against the lanes' own law fixtures, and presented the outputs side by side. Nine cases covering the full behavioral surface: normal description (identity control), keyArg path (identity control), multi-line description (fold — old produced 3 lines with ownerless continuation text, new produces 1), planted forgery via `] U:` (defang — old left a perfect fake speaker turn that the natural test showed laundered 2/2, new breaks the token shape), inline marker mimicry (defang), wrapper-tag close attack `&lt;/new_messages&gt;` (defang — old closes the builder's envelope early, new splits the terminator), media-family token (defang), heredoc keyArg &gt; 60 chars (the 4,368-raw-line class — old did truncate-then-nothing leaving a newline inside the 60-char cap breaking line protocol, new does fold-then-truncate so all 60 units are visible content), unicode whitespace (NEL+BOM fold pinned cross-lane, ZWSP deliberately NOT folded per Rust-parity law), W2 post-commit-strip re-forge (the subtle round-1 finding — old's hash extraction + space collapse REBUILT a raw marker after the sanitizer ran, new re-runs defang after the strip), and idempotence (`sanitize(sanitize(x)) == sanitize(x)`).

The guarantee stated: identity on every input without a builder token or line break; change only on classes that could break the line protocol or forge the grammar; fold set pinned rather than incidental; self-stable output. On today's real traffic only the whitespace/defang classes actually fired (815 lines / 24,556, drift inside model noise); the newline-forge class is latent.

U: "&lt;/new_messages\&gt;" i'm sure that it's a reproduction artifact, but shouldn't "&lt;" also be escaped?
I explained that backslash-before-terminator is load-bearing design, not style: (1) escaping `&lt;` wouldn't break the token — `\&lt;new_messages&gt;` still contains the literal substring `&lt;/new_messages&gt;` intact for any consumer doing literal match; splitting from the inside (one character before the final character) is the minimal edit that destroys the literal itself; (2) terminator placement makes the defang idempotent — `(&lt;\/new_messages&gt;)` requires `&gt;` directly after the name, `s\&gt;` doesn't match, so a second pass is a no-op; a `&lt;`-prefixed scheme fails this because `\&lt;new_messages&gt;` still contains a regex match, so every re-serve appends another backslash; (3) a bare `&lt;` carries no structural meaning to the historian or model — the token is the complete `&lt;tag&gt;` shape. Backslash over XML-escaping (`&lt;`) because this is a model-read transcript, not a rendering channel — nothing decodes entities, and the criterion is byte-non-identity plus readability plus cross-lane determinism (the Rust twin defangs identically, pinned by shared vectors file).

U: so sanitiser specifically targets xml tag instead of symbol?
I confirmed: it defangs a closed vocabulary of token shapes, not any symbol. Three regex families: turn markers (`] U:` `] A:` `] TC:` `] M:` → backslash before colon), media prefix (`[media:` → backslash before colon), envelope tags (only for `HISTORIAN_WRAPPER_TAGS` set → backslash before `&gt;`). A random `&lt;` or `&gt;` passes through untouched; an unlisted tag like `&lt;system&gt;` passes through too. The contract is "no pasted text may remain byte-identical to something the builder itself emits" — the impersonable set is exactly enumerable because the historian prompt quotes the exact template strings. The design is census-locked: the guarded set and the builder's emitted set are the same constants (`historian-wrapper-tags.ts` feeds both the wrapper open/close and the sanitizer regex), with a two-way law plus planted-rogue controls per shape.

U: it also seems like that if text doesn't have attack surface it's basically untouched
I clarified the two independent halves: defang is attack-surface-conditional (fires only on token match — 66 lines / 24,556 = 0.27% on real traffic); fold is content-independent (`[\s\u0085]+` → single space + trim runs on every spliced field, hostile or not, because newlines inside a `TC:` line break the line protocol whatever their intent). That's why measured drift was 3.3% (815 lines) not 0.27%: 439 benign-whitespace folds + 259 commit-shape re-extractions + 66 defangs. Text with no token, no newline, no tab/NEL/BOM, no repeated space — 96.7% — comes out byte-identical, asserted per-window against the unsanitized arm.

U: well, why sanitizer just don't add generally recognisable \n instead of eating newline completely
I gave four reasons in weight order: (1) the original never left the store — the sanitizer runs on the historian's input transcript, unmodified params live verbatim in the host DB addressable by tag/ordinal (`ctx_expand` gets them back with real newlines); the stub exists so the summarizer knows *that* a call happened, newline positions in a 60-char-truncated stub carry no fact the compartment summary needs; (2) `\n` is not a safe escape marker — model-authored description params routinely contain literal backslash-n text, so genuine newlines folding to `\n` makes the transcript ambiguous, requiring a full reversible codec inside a lossy display stub; (3) the consumer never decodes — the reader is an LLM at temperature 0.7 with no decoder for `\n` sequences; what the protocol requires is absence of structural bytes, not memory of them; (4) cross-lane parity was already pinned to space — the Rust lane's expansion path folded newlines to space before this lane existed, and the differential golden vectors require TS and Rust to agree byte-for-byte. Design line: lossless where the truth is stored, lossy only where a verbatim copy exists elsewhere.

