This splits the design into two artifacts with two instruments: the verdict (structured: span bounds + consumption citations, mechanically verified before apply) and the summary (prose rendering, quality measured by the fidelity-gradient validator). The verdict is where all the risk lives and is gated by a script; the summary is the historian's existing competency with different placement.

I ran a store census on my own session (the user pointed to it as evidence):
U: you can take a look at your own context window, i can recall at least two cases which most definitely still in the window

Results: ~78% of live-tail tool bytes are finished-arc mass. Two exhibits: (1) a 45,249 B `paseo_list_models` dump (64% of tail) used for exactly one decision hours ago, never referenced, never stamped; (2) two identical `list_models` dumps where the older is already `skeleton_real`-dropped but the newer (deader) copy is fully active — positional age-ranking can invert liveness. The self-report failure is mine: I'm stamp-briefed and never stamped the 45 KB because nothing after consuming it prompted a stamp.

I initially caveated this as "n=1, not typical." The user corrected:
