This establishes the fold-before-summary dependency (an unfolded arc can't be properly summarized because nothing has certified its span closed) and the three-mechanism non-substitution: mechanical compartments + historian summaries (primary, earned by the fold), arcs (segmentation + closure capability, not a new summary lane), LKG message compression (candidate replacement for summary bytes, gated by fidelity-gradient at model perspective, currently fails). Recorded as ruling in note #92.

The user then identified the statelessness gap:
U: the other problem is that historian works with a limited scope, e.g. it does not know what messages were summarised earlier and what arcs has been assigned

I identified two sub-problems (window-edge trust: an arc at the window's end may continue in the next chunk; islands: mid-window folds put builder-emitted summary blocks into future windows, and the historian has no concept of "sealed artifact, not source material") and proposed the fix shape: the model stays stateless, the store carries the memory — an arc ledger (one row per arc: id, span, status, title, owning compartment) surfaced into every pass as structured context, with a window-edge law (closure requires next-pass citations, never self-declared at the edge).

The user proposed a solution direction for the statelessness problem:
U: well, in theory we can just add some kind of recall tool for historian to look at compartment summaries and just attach following messages to exact the same arc if they does looks like continuation

This is a recall-tool approach to the islands/continuation problem — the historian queries existing compartment summaries and attaches continuation messages to the same arc if they look like a continuation. Not yet adjudicated against the ledger design; the conversation ends here.

## 23907-23917 · Settled arc mechanism design: two-agent split, pending merge, librarian-as-compiler
Continuation of the invizipburu v2 arc mechanism design conversation. I proposed the recall tool with three constitutional constraints: (1) it complements the ledger index (recovery-locality law — a model can't query what it doesn't know exists, so the compact structural index is injected, depth is paid on demand), (2) published arcs are immutable — continuation attaches only to unpublished arcs, a sealed arc gets a `parent_arc` reference rather than a rewrite, (3) recall is judgement-only (informs verdicts inside the pass, never on the serve path).

U: "in theory it could even expand not-applied compartments with a bit wider summary if they belong to exact the same arc, e.g. reduce context usage on next boundary move"

This produced ruling 7(e): the pre-applied/post-applied divide is the whole law — unapplied bytes are still deliberation, applied bytes are history. Adjacent pending compartments covering one arc merge into one coherent narrative before the boundary moves. Merge is a historian judgement (citing arc ledger: same arc_id, adjacent spans, both pending); the server applies only if mechanical checks pass. Same-pass decision, frozen at apply. Receipt per merge: source compartment ids, arc id, byte delta. Guardrail: merge before validation so the raw-bound validator sees final bytes only.

U: "but the routine case is post-history merging" thing is, if historians with recall mechanism could actually track continuity, "dreamer"(let's call him librarian) could just get all of the detected compartments at once and make summary for them

This reframed the consolidation agent (named "librarian") from detective to compiler. Continuity gets judged once, in context, while the material is still in the historian's hands — the historian sees the window, the index, and can recall the prior arc's summary to confirm same-story. The librarian groups sealed compartments by parent-chain component in the ledger, feeds each group through one summary pass, schedules the replacement. Two mechanical rules: eligibility (verdict closed + all compartments sealed + quiet horizon measured in pass count, not wall clock) and invalidation (new child attachment cancels the schedule, arc reopens, librarian recompiles later). The risky judgement (continuity) lives at fold time with full context; the batch judgement (narration) lives offline with bounded per-tree budget.

Net architecture: historian carries all judgement (segmentation, closure verdicts, continuity chaining via recall at fold time), librarian carries zero judgement (compile trees → summarize → schedule), server carries all authority (mechanical validation, receipts, boundary-only apply, cancellable pending rows). Replacing applied compartment blocks is the pre-authorized exception to the no-compaction-of-compaction law — structurally the same as the rotation exception, fenced by planted-rogue census.

