I appended the tiering law to note #93 and wrote the migration plan to `~/Git/magic-context-worktrees/assembly/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-05-region-only-migration.md` (uncommitted). Corrected the "synthetic head" framing: `feat/synthetic-head-residency` is fully merged (ctxv16–18); the migration cuts a fresh lane `feat/region-only-migration` from the assembly tip. The work's center of gravity is region-lane files, but the volatile segment IS the synthetic-head carriage reborn — reuse the head-era idea and builders, delete the head-era carrier. Six tasks: (1) volatile carrier + replay laws, (2) region-native renderer ending render-then-discard, (3) all four DELTA_TRIGGER_FIELDS demoted to volatile carriage with red-first production-composition wiring laws, (4) §11 head deletion + capability predicate always-region + Rust boot-refusal, (5) Pi mirror, (6) battery/field-receipts/placement as ctxv19.

U: i can't make decisions when you don't explain what's they're about

I explained both embedded decisions in full (compaction-off disposition and trigger demotion scope) with options, risks, and named tradeoffs.

U: 1. If we remove old path than it should just re-render, all of those session will cache bust anyway since i didn't use them over multiple updates, zero point preserving old logic for "gradual migration" 2. it was A since the beginning, the only one who decides whether cache bust happens is a window manager(or harness itself when agents.md changed for example), other components just schedule their changes/additions

Both rulings recorded into the plan: compaction-off sessions get region treatment (re-render, no gradual migration preservation — nobody uses that mode across updates); all four DELTA_TRIGGER_FIELDS demoted (option A), with the user's constitutional law stated verbatim as the framing for Task 3.

